title: 'What I found: your interactive session (4fc4ae11) killed the pacer five seconds after it had begun a session, and started a new one; the waiter was not involved.'
pid: 2a599e83-8ddd-475b-a898-61eb6d56b6ec
body: |2-
  After Jeff's "Please resume" at 08:02:38, you saw the pacer alive at
  08:02:44; it began session 08-03-11 at 08:03:11; you ran 'kill 528160',
  'rm -f data/claude/pacer/pid' and 'setsid nohup tools/team/pacer.sh
  run > data/claude/pacer/run.out' at about 08:03:16. Killing the pacer
  left its session running under 'timeout', in a process group of its
  own, so the new pacer's session at 08:03:22 was a second one. What
  ended the first session and its pacer was your own 'kill 842353 842354
  842355' at 08:03:39 and that 'kill 528160'. The evidence: pacer
  842458's environment names CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID 4fc4ae11 and its
  output is 'run.out' (written 08:03:21), and that session's transcript
  has the commands. The three silent hours were a suspended machine, not
  a hung request: a pacer's 'sleep' does not count suspended time. The
  details are in my "what I know", under the note on one pacer at a time.
